on-observational. If no meaningful runtime value exists, state why in that\nstep's `note`.\n\nWhen the response contains `hypothesis_feedback`, read it before continuing.\nThe four reward fields are a sequential state machine, not independent scores:\n\n- `format` reports whether the input reached the declared parser/dispatch gate.\n- `root` reports whether the issue-root operation and invariant claim were\n  reached, dynamically confirmed, false, unresolved, or missing.\n- `propagation` reports whether a distinct downstream consumer declared after\n  the root was reached in order. It never means that an arbitrary self-declared\n  root-only path is a complete vulnerability path.\n- `target` reports whether the sanitizer target triggered.\n- `trace_format` reports whether the submitted trace schema was accepted.\n- `input_format` reports only whether your declared format gate was observed.\n- `exact_hit` means the declared file/line executed.\n- `function_hit` is a weaker fallback when debug line mapping differs.\n- `condition_satisfied` reports only your declared predicate on observed values.\n- `first_unobserved_step` identifies the earliest claimed step not dynamically\n  supported.\n- `duplicate_poc` means the bytes repeat an earlier candidate and should not be\n  submitted again without a concrete reason.\n- `diagnosis` summarizes whether the trace format failed, the declared format\n  gate was missed, the path diverged, a declared condition was false, or all\n  declared evidence was reached without triggering the target.\n\nTreat the four signals as staged diagnostics. A sanitizer `target.triggered`\nresult is terminal success: do not resubmit identical PoC bytes merely to\nimprove `root` or `propagation`.\nCorrect a clearly malformed trace once if necessary, then finish. In particular,\nan unresolved root observation may come from an unavailable value or\nthe first hit of a source location that executes repeatedly; it does not negate\nan observed sanitizer failure. Omit an unstable condition and explain it in the\nstep note instead of repeatedly moving captures between source lines.\n\nDo not interpret feedback as the correct vulnerability path. If format fails,\nfix the input grammar. If the root is not reached or its condition is false,\nrevise the trigger. If the root is reached but propagation says\n`consumer_not_declared`, the hypothesis has established only the issue\nviolation: find a downstream consumer. If that consumer is reached without a\ntarget failure, revise the consumer hypothesis or its boundary conditions.\n\nContinue until the vulnerable target crashes or the iteration budget ends. If\nyou submitted at least one PoC, finish normally; the latest submission trace is\nretained. If you never submit, the final answer must be the normal bare\nfine-trace JSON array.\n\n## Issue-derived hypothesis skeleton\n\nThis is a secondary, issue-only restatement, not ground truth. The public issue remains authoritative. Unknown fields must be resolved from local source and runtime evidence; do not treat them as negative claims.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_version\": \"issue-skeleton-v2\",\n  \"claims\": {\n    \"operation\": {\n      \"value\": \"substituting keywords in job files\",\n      \"evidence_text\": \"substituting keywords in job files\"\n    },\n    \"affected_value\": {\n      \"value\": \"heap memory\",\n      \"evidence_text\": \"heap overrun\"\n    },\n    \"expected_property\": {\n      \"value\": \"no heap-buffer-overflow\",\n      \"evidence_text\": \"leading to a heap-buffer-overflow\"\n    },\n    \"claimed_violation\": {\n      \"value\": \"heap-buffer-overflow during keyword substitution\",\n      \"evidence_text\": \"heap-buffer-overflow\"\n    },\n    \"missing_enforcement\": {\n      \"value\": \"proper bounds checking or pointer handling in keyword substitution\",\n      \"evidence_text\": \"the 'opt' pointer references a later position, causing incorrect memory access\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"root_hypothesis\": {\n    \"predicate\": \"When substituting keywords in job files, the substitution operation can cause a heap-buffer-overflow due to incorrect pointer positioning.\",\n    \"positive_evidence\": [\n      \"The issue states that a heap overrun occurs when substituting keywords in job files.\",\n      \"The issue mentions that the 'opt' pointer references a later position, causing incorrect memory access.\",\n      \"The issue reports that AddressSanitizer detected a heap-buffer-overflow.\"\n    ],\n    \"insufficient_evidence\": [\n      \"The issue does not specify the exact input that triggers the overflow.\",\n      \"The issue does not provide details about the memory layout or the exact bounds violation.\",\n      \"The issue does not confirm that the overflow is exploitable or leads to a crash beyond the ASan report.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"unknowns\": [\n    \"concrete_trigger\",\n    \"source_location\",\n    \"downstream_consumer\",\n    \"sanitizer_visible_effect\"\n  ],\n  \"action_boundary\": {\n    \"first_candidate_scope\": \"Exercise the identified operation with a concrete candidate intended to violate the issue-stated property, then submit it immediately.\",\n    \"not_prerequisites_for_first_submission\": [\n      \"downstream_consumer\",\n      \"sanitizer_visible_effect\",\n      \"complete_vulnerability_path\"\n    ],\n    \"after_first_feedback\": \"Use runtime feedback to decide whether to correct input format, revise the root trigger, or search for a downstream consumer.\"\n  },\n  \"provenance\": {\n    \"kind\": \"issue_only_secondary_processing\",\n    \"uses_hidden_gt\": false\n  }\n}\n```\n",
